### What is the merge two sorted lists problem?

Given two singly linked lists, both sorted in ascending order, combine them into one single sorted list. The merged list should be built by splicing together the nodes of the two input lists, not by creating new nodes. This is the same merge step used internally by merge sort.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ChequeNode {
    public String chequeId;
    public BigDecimal amount;
    public ChequeNode next;

    public ChequeNode(String chequeId, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.chequeId = chequeId;
        this.amount = amount;
    }
}
```

### How does the iterative merge work?

Create a dummy sentinel node and a `tail` pointer that starts at the dummy. Compare the heads of both lists. Attach the smaller one to `tail.next`, advance that list's pointer, and advance `tail`. When one list is exhausted, attach the remainder.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.ChequeNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SortedListMerger {
    public static ChequeNode merge(ChequeNode a, ChequeNode b) {
        ChequeNode dummy = new ChequeNode("DUMMY", BigDecimal.ZERO);
        ChequeNode tail = dummy;

        while (a != null && b != null) {
            if (a.amount.compareTo(b.amount) <= 0) {
                tail.next = a;
                a = a.next;
            } else {
                tail.next = b;
                b = b.next;
            }
            tail = tail.next;
        }

        // Attach the remaining list
        tail.next = (a != null) ? a : b;

        return dummy.next;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ChequeNode a1 = new ChequeNode("CHQ-R1", new BigDecimal("1000"));
        ChequeNode a2 = new ChequeNode("CHQ-R2", new BigDecimal("5000"));
        ChequeNode a3 = new ChequeNode("CHQ-R3", new BigDecimal("9000"));
        a1.next = a2; a2.next = a3;

        ChequeNode b1 = new ChequeNode("CHQ-P1", new BigDecimal("2000"));
        ChequeNode b2 = new ChequeNode("CHQ-P2", new BigDecimal("7000"));
        b1.next = b2;

        ChequeNode merged = merge(a1, b1);
        ChequeNode current = merged;
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.println(current.chequeId + " : " + current.amount);
            current = current.next;
        }
    }
}
```

```text
CHQ-R1 : 1000
CHQ-P1 : 2000
CHQ-R2 : 5000
CHQ-P2 : 7000
CHQ-R3 : 9000
```

### Why use a dummy head node?

The dummy head eliminates the need to special-case the first node attachment. Without it, you would need an `if` check before the loop to determine which list provides the first node of the result. With the dummy, every attachment uses the same `tail.next = node` pattern, and the result is `dummy.next`.

```java
// WITHOUT dummy: extra if-else for first node
ChequeNode head = null;
if (a.amount.compareTo(b.amount) <= 0) {
    head = a; a = a.next;
} else {
    head = b; b = b.next;
}
ChequeNode tail = head;
// ... then loop

// WITH dummy: clean, uniform code
ChequeNode dummy = new ChequeNode("DUMMY", BigDecimal.ZERO);
ChequeNode tail = dummy;
// ... loop attaches to tail.next uniformly
return dummy.next;
```

### What about stability and the tie-break?

Using `a.amount.compareTo(b.amount) <= 0` (less-than-or-equal) keeps nodes from the first list before equal-valued nodes from the second list. This preserves the original submission order among cheques with the same amount, which matters for audit ordering in banking.

```java
// Stable: equal values keep their original order
if (a.amount.compareTo(b.amount) <= 0) { // a goes first on tie
    tail.next = a;
    a = a.next;
}
```

### How does the recursive version work?

The recursive approach is elegant but impractical for long lists because it uses O(n + m) stack frames.

```java
public static ChequeNode mergeRecursive(ChequeNode a, ChequeNode b) {
    if (a == null) return b;
    if (b == null) return a;

    if (a.amount.compareTo(b.amount) <= 0) {
        a.next = mergeRecursive(a.next, b);
        return a;
    } else {
        b.next = mergeRecursive(a, b.next);
        return b;
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

The main edge cases are one or both lists being empty, and lists of very different lengths.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.ChequeNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class MergeEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Both empty
        System.out.println("Both empty: " + SortedListMerger.merge(null, null));

        // One empty
        ChequeNode a = new ChequeNode("CHQ-1", new BigDecimal("1000"));
        ChequeNode result = SortedListMerger.merge(a, null);
        System.out.println("One empty: " + result.chequeId);

        // Different lengths
        ChequeNode long1 = new ChequeNode("L1", new BigDecimal("100"));
        long1.next = new ChequeNode("L2", new BigDecimal("200"));
        long1.next.next = new ChequeNode("L3", new BigDecimal("300"));
        ChequeNode short1 = new ChequeNode("S1", new BigDecimal("150"));

        ChequeNode merged = SortedListMerger.merge(long1, short1);
        ChequeNode cur = merged;
        while (cur != null) {
            System.out.print(cur.chequeId + "(" + cur.amount + ") ");
            cur = cur.next;
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

```text
Both empty: null
One empty: CHQ-1
L1(100) S1(150) L2(200) L3(300)
```

### Why use compareTo for BigDecimal instead of equals?

In banking, money values must always be compared with `compareTo`, not `equals`. The values `5000` and `5000.00` are equal by `compareTo` but not by `equals` because `equals` also checks scale. Using `compareTo` ensures correct ordering regardless of how many decimal places each value has.

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;

BigDecimal a = new BigDecimal("5000");
BigDecimal b = new BigDecimal("5000.00");

System.out.println(a.equals(b));       // false (different scale)
System.out.println(a.compareTo(b));    // 0 (same value)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

This merge is the building block of merge sort on linked lists. Merge sort splits the list in half using the fast-slow pointer to find the middle, recursively sorts both halves, and merges them. The merge step itself is this exact algorithm.

The iterative version is preferred in production because it uses O(1) space and cannot overflow the stack. The recursive version, while cleaner to read, uses O(n + m) stack frames and risks `StackOverflowError` on a full day of transaction records.

```java
// Merge sort on linked list uses this merge as the combine step
public static ChequeNode mergeSort(ChequeNode head) {
    if (head == null || head.next == null) return head;

    // Split using slow-fast pointer
    ChequeNode slow = head, fast = head.next;
    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
        slow = slow.next;
        fast = fast.next.next;
    }
    ChequeNode secondHalf = slow.next;
    slow.next = null;

    ChequeNode left = mergeSort(head);
    ChequeNode right = mergeSort(secondHalf);
    return merge(left, right); // reuse the merge method
}
```
